import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { ArrowRight, ArrowUpRight, WavesHorizontal as Waves, Wind, Activity, Info } from '@lucide/astro';
---
<Layout title="ENSO & Atlantic Hurricanes" active="hurricanes"><div class="page-shell subpage"><div class="page-intro"><div><span class="eyebrow">PACIFIC SIGNAL · ATLANTIC RESPONSE</span><h1>One ocean's signal.<br /><span>Another ocean's season.</span></h1><p>How ENSO can shape the environment for Atlantic hurricanes.</p></div></div><section class="connection-flow" aria-label="El Niño can increase Atlantic wind shear and make hurricane development less favorable"><div><Waves size={29} /><span class="eyebrow">TROPICAL PACIFIC</span><h2>El Niño</h2><p>A shift in ocean and atmospheric conditions.</p></div><ArrowRight class="flow-arrow" size={25} /><div><Wind size={29} /><span class="eyebrow">ATLANTIC ENVIRONMENT</span><h2>More wind shear</h2><p>Often stronger changes in wind with height.</p></div><ArrowRight class="flow-arrow" size={25} /><div><Activity size={29} /><span class="eyebrow">HURRICANE DEVELOPMENT</span><h2>Less favorable</h2><p>A broad tendency, not a storm prediction.</p></div></section><section class="explanation-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE CONNECTION</span><h2>Wind shear can disrupt a storm.</h2><p>El Niño often favors increased vertical wind shear over parts of the Atlantic hurricane development region. La Niña can favor reduced shear. Other ingredients, including Atlantic ocean temperatures and moisture, also matter.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">THE LIMIT</span><h2>Fewer storms does not mean no risk.</h2><p>ENSO does not determine the track, strength, or landfall of an individual storm. A quieter basin-wide season can still produce a destructive hurricane.</p></div></section><div class="content-notice"><Info size={19} /><div><strong>Use official storm information for decisions.</strong><p>This page describes climate context. It does not provide warnings, evacuation advice, or a current seasonal hurricane forecast.</p></div></div><div class="source-callout"><Waves size={22} /><div><strong>Follow the current Atlantic storm picture.</strong><p>Current advisories and warnings are published by the National Hurricane Center.</p></div><a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open NHC <ArrowUpRight size={15} /></a></div><div class="related-station"><span class="eyebrow">FROM THE SAME INDEPENDENT BUILDER</span><h3>Following a particular storm?</h3><a href="https://www.hurricanetracker.cc/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Visit Hurricane Tracker <ArrowUpRight size={16} /></a></div></div></Layout>
